    {
      "Id": "edc4bb5d",
      "Timestamp": "2026-10-20T22:25:00.3177696-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "\u0060\u0060\u0060\n[22:23:03.830] [SYS] Starting Automation Server on port 9999...\n[22:23:04.426] [SYS] Client Connected: bot_x2zfzb3rb via HttpPolling\n[22:23:06.147] [SYS] Fetching DOM from bot_x2zfzb3rb...\n[22:23:07.416] [ERR] Failed to fetch DOM: The JSON value could not be converted to TestBedApp.Models.DomNode. Path: $ | LineNumber: 0 | BytePositionInLine: 1.\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nOk so we need to fix this issue with the Refresh DOM operation.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe issue occurs because the generated Tampermonkey script was casting the \u0060output\u0060 payload to a string using \u0060String(output)\u0060. For JavaScript objects (like the serialized DOM tree), this results in the literal string \u0060\u0022[object Object]\u0022\u0060. When the C# application attempts to deserialize \u0060\u0022[object Object]\u0022\u0060 into a \u0060DomNode\u0060, it throws a \u0060JsonException\u0060 because it is invalid JSON.\n\nI have updated the \u0060sendResult\u0060 functions in \u0060ScriptGenerator.cs\u0060 for both HTTP Polling and WebSocket modes to check if the output is an object. If it is, it will use \u0060JSON.stringify(output)\u0060 to properly serialize it; otherwise, it falls back to \u0060String(output)\u0060.\n\nTo test:\n\n- Run \u0060TestBedApp\u0060.\n- Generate and copy the new script, then update it in Tampermonkey.\n- Refresh the target page so the new script connects.\n- Go to the \u0022DOM Explorer\u0022 tab and click \u0022Refresh DOM\u0022. The tree should now populate successfully without throwing a JSON conversion error.\n\n--CODESTART--\n\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\\Generators\\ScriptGenerator.cs**\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n    async function sendResult(taskId, status, output) {\n        await tmFetch(\u0060${API_BASE}/result/${INSTANCE_ID}\u0060, {\n            method: \u0027POST\u0027,\n            body: JSON.stringify({ task_id: taskId, status: status, output: String(output) })\n        });\n    }\n==== REPLACE\n    async function sendResult(taskId, status, output) {\n        const outStr = typeof output === \u0027object\u0027 ? JSON.stringify(output) : String(output);\n        await tmFetch(\u0060${API_BASE}/result/${INSTANCE_ID}\u0060, {\n            method: \u0027POST\u0027,\n            body: JSON.stringify({ task_id: taskId, status: status, output: outStr })\n        });\n    }\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n    function sendResult(taskId, status, output) {\n        if (ws \u0026\u0026 ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {\n            ws.send(JSON.stringify({ task_id: taskId, status: status, output: String(output) }));\n        }\n    }\n==== REPLACE\n    function sendResult(taskId, status, output) {\n        if (ws \u0026\u0026 ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {\n            const outStr = typeof output === \u0027object\u0027 ? JSON.stringify(output) : String(output);\n            ws.send(JSON.stringify({ task_id: taskId, status: status, output: outStr }));\n        }\n    }\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The issue occurs because the generated Tampermonkey script was casting the \u0060output\u0060 payload to a string using \u0060String(output)\u0060. For JavaScript objects (like the serialized DOM tree), this results in the literal string \u0060\u0022[object Object]\u0022\u0060. When the C# application attempts to deserialize \u0060\u0022[object Object]\u0022\u0060 into a \u0060DomNode\u0060, it throws a \u0060JsonException\u0060 because it is invalid JSON.\n\nI have updated the \u0060sendResult\u0060 functions in \u0060ScriptGenerator.cs\u0060 for both HTTP Polling and WebSocket modes to check if the output is an object. If it is, it will use \u0060JSON.stringify(output)\u0060 to properly serialize it; otherwise, it falls back to \u0060String(output)\u0060.\n\nTo test:\n\n- Run \u0060TestBedApp\u0060.\n- Generate and copy the new script, then update it in Tampermonkey.\n- Refresh the target page so the new script connects.\n- Go to the \u0022DOM Explorer\u0022 tab and click \u0022Refresh DOM\u0022. The tree should now populate successfully without throwing a JSON conversion error.",
