    {
      "Id": "7cb5407f",
      "Timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:05:50.1598762-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "[Attached Image]\n\n[Attached Image]\n\n[Attached Image]\n\nOk now we need to fix this final bug in the dom tree viewer control. Notice these screenshots and how the highlight when we are selected on an element either will extend too far past the final character of the element thats rendered, or it will fall short. We need to fix this so that the highlight square extends properly over the last character and no longer or shorter for all elements.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe issue occurs because the default \u0060e.Bounds\u0060 width provided by the \u0060TreeView\u0060 is calculated based on the underlying \u0060TreeNode.Text\u0060 property. In \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060, the \u0060TreeNode.Text\u0060 is generated to always include the \u0060...\u003C/tag\u003E\u0060 suffix for elements. However, the custom drawing logic in \u0060DomTreeView.cs\u0060 conditionally omits this suffix if the node is expanded or has no children. This mismatch causes the background highlight to be wider than the actually drawn text.\n\nTo fix this, I have refactored the \u0060OnDrawNode\u0060 method to first build a list of the exact text segments it intends to draw. It then measures the total width of these segments to draw a perfectly sized background rectangle before drawing the text over it.\n\nTo test these changes:\n\n- Run the \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 and load a DOM tree.\n- Select various nodes (expanded elements, collapsed elements, and text nodes).\n- Verify that the blue selection highlight now perfectly bounds the rendered text without extending too far or falling short.\n\n--CODESTART--\n\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\TestBedApp\\Controls\\DomTreeView.cs**\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n            bool isSelected = (e.State \u0026 TreeNodeStates.Selected) != 0;\n            \n            using (var bgBrush = new SolidBrush(isSelected ? _selBgColor : BackColor))\n            {\n                e.Graphics.FillRectangle(bgBrush, e.Bounds);\n            }\n\n            using (var format = new StringFormat(StringFormat.GenericTypographic))\n            {\n                format.FormatFlags |= StringFormatFlags.MeasureTrailingSpaces | StringFormatFlags.NoWrap;\n\n                if (e.Node.Tag is DomNode domNode)\n                {\n                    float x = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                    float y = e.Bounds.Top \u002B ((e.Bounds.Height - _codeFont.Height) / 2f);\n\n                    void Draw(string text, Color color)\n                    {\n                        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(text)) return;\n                        using (var brush = new SolidBrush(color))\n                        {\n                            e.Graphics.DrawString(text, _codeFont, brush, x, y, format);\n                        }\n                        x \u002B= e.Graphics.MeasureString(text, _codeFont, new PointF(x, y), format).Width;\n                    }\n\n                    if (domNode.Type == \u0022element\u0022)\n                    {\n                        Draw(\u0022\u003C\u0022, _punctColor);\n                        Draw(domNode.TagName, _tagColor);\n\n                        if (domNode.Attributes != null)\n                        {\n                            foreach (var attr in domNode.Attributes)\n                            {\n                                Draw(\u0022 \u0022, _textColor);\n                                Draw(attr.Key, _attrNameColor);\n                                Draw(\u0022=\u0022, _punctColor);\n                                Draw($\u0022\\\u0022{attr.Value}\\\u0022\u0022, _attrValueColor);\n                            }\n                        }\n\n                        Draw(\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor);\n\n                        if (e.Node.Nodes.Count \u003E 0 \u0026\u0026 !e.Node.IsExpanded)\n                        {\n                            Draw(\u0022...\u0022, _punctColor);\n                            Draw($\u0022\u003C/\u0022, _punctColor);\n                            Draw(domNode.TagName, _tagColor);\n                            Draw(\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor);\n                        }\n                    }\n                    else if (domNode.Type == \u0022text\u0022)\n                    {\n                        Draw($\u0022\\\u0022{domNode.Text}\\\u0022\u0022, _textColor);\n                    }\n                }\n                else\n                {\n                    float y = e.Bounds.Top \u002B ((e.Bounds.Height - _codeFont.Height) / 2f);\n                    using (var brush = new SolidBrush(isSelected ? Color.White : _textColor))\n                    {\n                        e.Graphics.DrawString(e.Node.Text, _codeFont, brush, e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2, y, format);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n==== REPLACE\n            bool isSelected = (e.State \u0026 TreeNodeStates.Selected) != 0;\n\n            using (var format = new StringFormat(StringFormat.GenericTypographic))\n            {\n                format.FormatFlags |= StringFormatFlags.MeasureTrailingSpaces | StringFormatFlags.NoWrap;\n\n                float y = e.Bounds.Top \u002B ((e.Bounds.Height - _codeFont.Height) / 2f);\n                var parts = new System.Collections.Generic.List\u003C(string text, Color color)\u003E();\n\n                if (e.Node.Tag is DomNode domNode)\n                {\n                    if (domNode.Type == \u0022element\u0022)\n                    {\n                        parts.Add((\u0022\u003C\u0022, _punctColor));\n                        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(domNode.TagName))\n                            parts.Add((domNode.TagName, _tagColor));\n\n                        if (domNode.Attributes != null)\n                        {\n                            foreach (var attr in domNode.Attributes)\n                            {\n                                parts.Add((\u0022 \u0022, _textColor));\n                                parts.Add((attr.Key, _attrNameColor));\n                                parts.Add((\u0022=\u0022, _punctColor));\n                                parts.Add(($\u0022\\\u0022{attr.Value}\\\u0022\u0022, _attrValueColor));\n                            }\n                        }\n\n                        parts.Add((\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor));\n\n                        if (e.Node.Nodes.Count \u003E 0 \u0026\u0026 !e.Node.IsExpanded)\n                        {\n                            parts.Add((\u0022...\u0022, _punctColor));\n                            parts.Add((\u0022\u003C/\u0022, _punctColor));\n                            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(domNode.TagName))\n                                parts.Add((domNode.TagName, _tagColor));\n                            parts.Add((\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor));\n                        }\n                    }\n                    else if (domNode.Type == \u0022text\u0022)\n                    {\n                        parts.Add(($\u0022\\\u0022{domNode.Text}\\\u0022\u0022, _textColor));\n                    }\n                }\n                else\n                {\n                    parts.Add((e.Node.Text, isSelected ? Color.White : _textColor));\n                }\n\n                // Measure total width\n                float totalWidth = 0;\n                float tempX = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                foreach (var part in parts)\n                {\n                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(part.text)) continue;\n                    float w = e.Graphics.MeasureString(part.text, _codeFont, new PointF(tempX, y), format).Width;\n                    totalWidth \u002B= w;\n                    tempX \u002B= w;\n                }\n\n                // Draw background\n                RectangleF bgRect = new RectangleF(e.Bounds.Left, e.Bounds.Top, totalWidth \u002B 4, e.Bounds.Height);\n                using (var bgBrush = new SolidBrush(isSelected ? _selBgColor : BackColor))\n                {\n                    e.Graphics.FillRectangle(bgBrush, bgRect);\n                }\n\n                // Draw text\n                float x = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                foreach (var part in parts)\n                {\n                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(part.text)) continue;\n                    using (var brush = new SolidBrush(part.color))\n                    {\n                        e.Graphics.DrawString(part.text, _codeFont, brush, x, y, format);\n                    }\n                    x \u002B= e.Graphics.MeasureString(part.text, _codeFont, new PointF(x, y), format).Width;\n                }\n            }\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The issue occurs because the default \u0060e.Bounds\u0060 width provided by the \u0060TreeView\u0060 is calculated based on the underlying \u0060TreeNode.Text\u0060 property. In \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060, the \u0060TreeNode.Text\u0060 is generated to always include the \u0060...\u003C/tag\u003E\u0060 suffix for elements. However, the custom drawing logic in \u0060DomTreeView.cs\u0060 conditionally omits this suffix if the node is expanded or has no children. This mismatch causes the background highlight to be wider than the actually drawn text.\n\nTo fix this, I have refactored the \u0060OnDrawNode\u0060 method to first build a list of the exact text segments it intends to draw. It then measures the total width of these segments to draw a perfectly sized background rectangle before drawing the text over it.\n\nTo test these changes:\n\n- Run the \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 and load a DOM tree.\n- Select various nodes (expanded elements, collapsed elements, and text nodes).\n- Verify that the blue selection highlight now perfectly bounds the rendered text without extending too far or falling short.",
      "Diffs": [
