      "Timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:09:45.5603184-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "[Attached Image]\n\n[Attached Image: image.png]\n![image.png](/api/media/2335f1c0dffd4c079beb322c097adf25)\n\nThis is great! Now for some reason, when i click on this element, and then click on the element below it, a stray div clsing tag is rendered and then highlighted on the parent element. This should not happen ever. \nI also want you to work on reducing the flicker of the control and make it perform much better at rendereing.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe stray closing tag artifact occurs because when a node is expanded, our custom drawing logic omits the \u0060...\u003C/div\u003E\u0060 suffix, making the rendered text shorter. However, the background fill was only covering this new, shorter width, leaving the old pixels from the collapsed state on the screen. I have fixed this by explicitly clearing the entire row width with the background color before drawing the new text and selection highlight.\n\nTo fix the flickering and improve rendering performance, I have added the \u0060WS_EX_COMPOSITED\u0060 (0x02000000) flag to the control\u0027s \u0060CreateParams.ExStyle\u0060. This is a native Windows feature that forces the control to paint its children to a double buffer before rendering them to the screen, which drastically reduces flickering in WinForms \u0060TreeView\u0060 controls.\n\nTo test these changes:\n\n- Run the \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 and load a DOM tree.\n- Expand and collapse nodes, and click between them. Verify that no stray \u0060\u003C/div\u003E\u0060 tags or text artifacts are left behind.\n- Scroll rapidly and resize the window to verify that the \u0060TreeView\u0060 no longer flickers.\n\n--CODESTART--\n\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\TestBedApp\\Controls\\DomTreeView.cs**\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n        protected override CreateParams CreateParams\n        {\n            get\n            {\n                CreateParams cp = base.CreateParams;\n                cp.Style |= 0x0080; // TVS_NOTOOLTIPS\n                return cp;\n            }\n        }\n==== REPLACE\n        protected override CreateParams CreateParams\n        {\n            get\n            {\n                CreateParams cp = base.CreateParams;\n                cp.Style |= 0x0080; // TVS_NOTOOLTIPS\n                cp.ExStyle |= 0x02000000; // WS_EX_COMPOSITED (Double buffering to prevent flicker)\n                return cp;\n            }\n        }\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n        protected override void OnDrawNode(DrawTreeNodeEventArgs e)\n        {\n            if (e.Node == null || e.Graphics == null) return;\n\n            // Fix for WinForms TreeView animation bug where nodes are drawn at the top left\n            if (!e.Node.IsVisible || e.Bounds.IsEmpty || e.Bounds.Y != e.Node.Bounds.Y) return;\n\n            // Use GDI\u002B drawing to prevent animation bleeding issues\n            e.Graphics.TextRenderingHint = System.Drawing.Text.TextRenderingHint.ClearTypeGridFit;\n\n            bool isSelected = (e.State \u0026 TreeNodeStates.Selected) != 0;\n\n            using (var format = new StringFormat(StringFormat.GenericTypographic))\n            {\n                format.FormatFlags |= StringFormatFlags.MeasureTrailingSpaces | StringFormatFlags.NoWrap;\n\n                float y = e.Bounds.Top \u002B ((e.Bounds.Height - _codeFont.Height) / 2f);\n                var parts = new System.Collections.Generic.List\u003C(string text, Color color)\u003E();\n\n                if (e.Node.Tag is DomNode domNode)\n                {\n                    if (domNode.Type == \u0022element\u0022)\n                    {\n                        parts.Add((\u0022\u003C\u0022, _punctColor));\n                        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(domNode.TagName))\n                            parts.Add((domNode.TagName, _tagColor));\n\n                        if (domNode.Attributes != null)\n                        {\n                            foreach (var attr in domNode.Attributes)\n                            {\n                                parts.Add((\u0022 \u0022, _textColor));\n                                parts.Add((attr.Key, _attrNameColor));\n                                parts.Add((\u0022=\u0022, _punctColor));\n                                parts.Add(($\u0022\\\u0022{attr.Value}\\\u0022\u0022, _attrValueColor));\n                            }\n                        }\n\n                        parts.Add((\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor));\n\n                        if (e.Node.Nodes.Count \u003E 0 \u0026\u0026 !e.Node.IsExpanded)\n                        {\n                            parts.Add((\u0022...\u0022, _punctColor));\n                            parts.Add((\u0022\u003C/\u0022, _punctColor));\n                            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(domNode.TagName))\n                                parts.Add((domNode.TagName, _tagColor));\n                            parts.Add((\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor));\n                        }\n                    }\n                    else if (domNode.Type == \u0022text\u0022)\n                    {\n                        parts.Add(($\u0022\\\u0022{domNode.Text}\\\u0022\u0022, _textColor));\n                    }\n                }\n                else\n                {\n                    parts.Add((e.Node.Text, isSelected ? Color.White : _textColor));\n                }\n\n                // Measure total width\n                float totalWidth = 0;\n                float tempX = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                foreach (var part in parts)\n                {\n                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(part.text)) continue;\n                    float w = e.Graphics.MeasureString(part.text, _codeFont, new PointF(tempX, y), format).Width;\n                    totalWidth \u002B= w;\n                    tempX \u002B= w;\n                }\n\n                // Draw background\n                RectangleF bgRect = new RectangleF(e.Bounds.Left, e.Bounds.Top, totalWidth \u002B 4, e.Bounds.Height);\n                using (var bgBrush = new SolidBrush(isSelected ? _selBgColor : BackColor))\n                {\n                    e.Graphics.FillRectangle(bgBrush, bgRect);\n                }\n\n                // Draw text\n                float x = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                foreach (var part in parts)\n                {\n                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(part.text)) continue;\n                    using (var brush = new SolidBrush(part.color))\n                    {\n                        e.Graphics.DrawString(part.text, _codeFont, brush, x, y, format);\n                    }\n                    x \u002B= e.Graphics.MeasureString(part.text, _codeFont, new PointF(x, y), format).Width;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n==== REPLACE\n        protected override void OnDrawNode(DrawTreeNodeEventArgs e)\n        {\n            if (e.Node == null || e.Graphics == null) return;\n\n            // Fix for WinForms TreeView animation bug where nodes are drawn at the top left\n            if (!e.Node.IsVisible || e.Bounds.IsEmpty || e.Bounds.Y != e.Node.Bounds.Y) return;\n\n            // Use GDI\u002B drawing to prevent animation bleeding issues\n            e.Graphics.TextRenderingHint = System.Drawing.Text.TextRenderingHint.ClearTypeGridFit;\n\n            bool isSelected = (e.State \u0026 TreeNodeStates.Selected) != 0;\n\n            // Clear the background to prevent artifacts from previous longer text states (e.g., when expanding/collapsing)\n            using (var clearBrush = new SolidBrush(BackColor))\n            {\n                int clearWidth = Math.Max(e.Bounds.Width, ClientSize.Width);\n                e.Graphics.FillRectangle(clearBrush, new Rectangle(e.Bounds.Left, e.Bounds.Top, clearWidth, e.Bounds.Height));\n            }\n\n            using (var format = new StringFormat(StringFormat.GenericTypographic))\n            {\n                format.FormatFlags |= StringFormatFlags.MeasureTrailingSpaces | StringFormatFlags.NoWrap;\n\n                float y = e.Bounds.Top \u002B ((e.Bounds.Height - _codeFont.Height) / 2f);\n                var parts = new System.Collections.Generic.List\u003C(string text, Color color)\u003E();\n\n                if (e.Node.Tag is DomNode domNode)\n                {\n                    if (domNode.Type == \u0022element\u0022)\n                    {\n                        parts.Add((\u0022\u003C\u0022, _punctColor));\n                        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(domNode.TagName))\n                            parts.Add((domNode.TagName, _tagColor));\n\n                        if (domNode.Attributes != null)\n                        {\n                            foreach (var attr in domNode.Attributes)\n                            {\n                                parts.Add((\u0022 \u0022, _textColor));\n                                parts.Add((attr.Key, _attrNameColor));\n                                parts.Add((\u0022=\u0022, _punctColor));\n                                parts.Add(($\u0022\\\u0022{attr.Value}\\\u0022\u0022, _attrValueColor));\n                            }\n                        }\n\n                        parts.Add((\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor));\n\n                        if (e.Node.Nodes.Count \u003E 0 \u0026\u0026 !e.Node.IsExpanded)\n                        {\n                            parts.Add((\u0022...\u0022, _punctColor));\n                            parts.Add((\u0022\u003C/\u0022, _punctColor));\n                            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(domNode.TagName))\n                                parts.Add((domNode.TagName, _tagColor));\n                            parts.Add((\u0022\u003E\u0022, _punctColor));\n                        }\n                    }\n                    else if (domNode.Type == \u0022text\u0022)\n                    {\n                        parts.Add(($\u0022\\\u0022{domNode.Text}\\\u0022\u0022, _textColor));\n                    }\n                }\n                else\n                {\n                    parts.Add((e.Node.Text, isSelected ? Color.White : _textColor));\n                }\n\n                // Measure total width\n                float totalWidth = 0;\n                float tempX = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                foreach (var part in parts)\n                {\n                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(part.text)) continue;\n                    float w = e.Graphics.MeasureString(part.text, _codeFont, new PointF(tempX, y), format).Width;\n                    totalWidth \u002B= w;\n                    tempX \u002B= w;\n                }\n\n                // Draw selection background\n                if (isSelected)\n                {\n                    RectangleF bgRect = new RectangleF(e.Bounds.Left, e.Bounds.Top, totalWidth \u002B 4, e.Bounds.Height);\n                    using (var selBrush = new SolidBrush(_selBgColor))\n                    {\n                        e.Graphics.FillRectangle(selBrush, bgRect);\n                    }\n                }\n\n                // Draw text\n                float x = e.Bounds.Left \u002B 2;\n                foreach (var part in parts)\n                {\n                    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(part.text)) continue;\n                    using (var brush = new SolidBrush(part.color))\n                    {\n                        e.Graphics.DrawString(part.text, _codeFont, brush, x, y, format);\n                    }\n                    x \u002B= e.Graphics.MeasureString(part.text, _codeFont, new PointF(x, y), format).Width;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The stray closing tag artifact occurs because when a node is expanded, our custom drawing logic omits the \u0060...\u003C/div\u003E\u0060 suffix, making the rendered text shorter. However, the background fill was only covering this new, shorter width, leaving the old pixels from the collapsed state on the screen. I have fixed this by explicitly clearing the entire row width with the background color before drawing the new text and selection highlight.\n\nTo fix the flickering and improve rendering performance, I have added the \u0060WS_EX_COMPOSITED\u0060 (0x02000000) flag to the control\u0027s \u0060CreateParams.ExStyle\u0060. This is a native Windows feature that forces the control to paint its children to a double buffer before rendering them to the screen, which drastically reduces flickering in WinForms \u0060TreeView\u0060 controls.\n\nTo test these changes:\n\n- Run the \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 and load a DOM tree.\n- Expand and collapse nodes, and click between them. Verify that no stray \u0060\u003C/div\u003E\u0060 tags or text artifacts are left behind.\n- Scroll rapidly and resize the window to verify that the \u0060TreeView\u0060 no longer flickers.",
