      "UserPrompt": "OK so now we need to fix the test for the eval js feature. Take a look at the logs and try to fix whats going wrong. \n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n[00:51:25.027] [TX -\u003E bot_tignb4mrt] EVAL | Code: console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\n[00:51:25.787] [ERR] Illegal return statement\n[00:51:35.141] [TX -\u003E bot_tignb4mrt] EVAL | Code: console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027);\n[00:51:36.148] [RX \u003C- bot_tignb4mrt] Status: completed | Output: undefined\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe \u0060Illegal return statement\u0060 error occurs because the JavaScript \u0060eval()\u0060 function executes code in the current scope, and \u0060return\u0060 statements are only valid inside functions.\n\nTo fix this, I have updated the \u0060EVAL\u0060 action handler in the \u0060ScriptGenerator\u0060 to wrap the incoming code inside an asynchronous Immediately Invoked Function Expression (IIFE) using the \u0060Function\u0060 constructor. This not only allows the use of \u0060return\u0060 sta",
      "ModelExplanation": "The \u0060Illegal return statement\u0060 error occurs because the JavaScript \u0060eval()\u0060 function executes code in the current scope, and \u0060return\u0060 statements are only valid inside functions.\n\nTo fix this, I have updated the \u0060EVAL\u0060 action handler in the \u0060ScriptGenerator\u0060 to wrap the incoming code inside an asynchronous Immediately Invoked Function Expression (IIFE) using the \u0060Function\u0060 constructor. This not only allows the use of \u0060return\u0060 sta",
