    {
      "Id": "2bb2a1a3",
      "Timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:38:37.0419249-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\r\n\r\nThe \u0060Illegal return statement\u0060 error occurs because the JavaScript \u0060eval()\u0060 function executes code in the current scope, and \u0060return\u0060 statements are only valid inside functions. \r\n\r\nTo fix this, I have updated the \u0060EVAL\u0060 action handler in the \u0060ScriptGenerator\u0060 to wrap the incoming code inside an asynchronous Immediately Invoked Function Expression (IIFE) using the \u0060Function\u0060 constructor. This not only allows the use of \u0060return\u0060 statements but also enables the use of \u0060await\u0060 inside the evaluated code.\r\n\r\nTo test these changes:\r\n1. Run the \u0060TestBedApp\u0060.\r\n2. Click **Generate \u0026 Copy Script** to generate the updated Tampermonkey script.\r\n3. Update the script in your Tampermonkey extension and reload the target web page.\r\n4. Send the \u0060EVAL\u0060 command with \u0060console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\u0060 again. It should now return \u0060Status: completed | Output: Success\u0060.\r\n\r\n--CODESTART--\r\n\r\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\\Generators\\ScriptGenerator.cs**\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\r\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\r\n    registerAction(\u0027EVAL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\r\n        return eval(payload.code);\r\n    });\r\n==== REPLACE\r\n    registerAction(\u0027EVAL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\r\n        const func = new Function(\u0060return (async () =\u003E { ${payload.code} })();\u0060);\r\n        return await func();\r\n    });\r\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The \u0060Illegal return statement\u0060 error occurs because the JavaScript \u0060eval()\u0060 function executes code in the current scope, and \u0060return\u0060 statements are only valid inside functions. \r\n\r\nTo fix this, I have updated the \u0060EVAL\u0060 action handler in the \u0060ScriptGenerator\u0060 to wrap the incoming code inside an asynchronous Immediately Invoked Function Expression (IIFE) using the \u0060Function\u0060 constructor. This not only allows the use of \u0060return\u0060 statements but also enables the use of \u0060await\u0060 inside the evaluated code.\r\n\r\nTo test these changes:\r\n1. Run the \u0060TestBedApp\u0060.\r\n2. Click **Generate \u0026 Copy Script** to generate the updated Tampermonkey script.\r\n3. Update the script in your Tampermonkey extension and reload the target web page.\r\n4. Send the \u0060EVAL\u0060 command with \u0060console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\u0060 again. It should now return \u0060Status: completed | Output: Success\u0060.",
