      "UserPrompt": "The root directory for this project is: C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\nThe following directories are currently tracked/active for this project (place new files in these directories unless otherwise specified):\n- C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\n- C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\TestBedApp\n\nFor some reason, the test eval feature does not work. It keeps saying illegal return statment, and then when i remove the return statment, it says output undefined.\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n[02:39:02.837] [SYS] Starting Automation Server on port 9999...\n[02:39:03.056] [SYS] Client Connected: bot_tignb4mrt via HttpPolling\n[02:39:06.597] [TX -\u003E bot_tignb4mrt] EVAL | Code: console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\n[02:39:07.947] [ERR] Illegal return statement\n[02:39:18.110] [TX -\u003E bot_tignb4mrt] EVAL | Code: console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027);\n[02:39:18.488] [RX \u003C- bot_tignb4mrt] Status: completed | Output: undefined\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe \u0022Illegal return statement\u0022 error occurs because the previous implementation used \u0060new Function\u0060 with a string that started with \u0060return\u0060. If the target website overrides or hooks the \u0060Function\u0060 constructor (a common anti-bot or CSP-enforcement technique) to evaluate code via \u0060eval()\u0060, the \u0060return\u0060 keyword ends up at the top level of the \u0060eval\u0060 context, which throws a \u0060SyntaxError\u0060.\n\nTo fix this and properly support \u0060await\u0060 and \u0060return\u0060 statements within the evaluated code, we can dynamically instantiate an \u0060AsyncFunction\u0060 instead. This natively parses the payload as the body of an asynchronous function, avoiding the need for an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) wrapper and bypassing basic \u0060Function\u0060 hooks.\n\n### How to test:\n\n- Click **Generate \u0026 Copy Script** in the TestBedApp and update your Tampermonkey script.\n- Reload the target page to connect the updated client.\n- Send the payload \u0060console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\u0060 via the **Test Eval (JS)** button. It should now correctly return \u0060Success\u0060 without throwing an error.\n\n--CODESTART--\n\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\\Generators\\ScriptGenerator.cs**\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n    registerAction(\u0027EVAL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\n        const func = new Function(\u0060return (async () =\u003E { ${payload.code} })();\u0060);\n        return await func();\n    });\n==== REPLACE\n    registerAction(\u0027EVAL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\n        const AsyncFunction = Object.getPrototypeOf(async function(){}).constructor;\n        const func = new AsyncFunction(payload.code);\n        return await func();\n    });\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The \u0022Illegal return statement\u0022 error occurs because the previous implementation used \u0060new Function\u0060 with a string that started with \u0060return\u0060. If the target website overrides or hooks the \u0060Function\u0060 constructor (a common anti-bot or CSP-enforcement technique) to evaluate code via \u0060eval()\u0060, the \u0060return\u0060 keyword ends up at the top level of the \u0060eval\u0060 context, which throws a \u0060SyntaxError\u0060.\n\nTo fix this and properly support \u0060await\u0060 and \u0060return\u0060 statements within the evaluated code, we can dynamically instantiate an \u0060AsyncFunction\u0060 instead. This natively parses the payload as the body of an asynchronous function, avoiding the need for an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) wrapper and bypassing basic \u0060Function\u0060 hooks.\n\n### How to test:\n\n- Click **Generate \u0026 Copy Script** in the TestBedApp and update your Tampermonkey script.\n- Reload the target page to connect the updated client.\n- Send the payload \u0060console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\u0060 via the **Test Eval (JS)** button. It should now correctly return \u0060Success\u0060 without throwing an error.",
