    {
      "Id": "bf7f2ca2",
      "Timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:46:11.8765884-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "This still didnt work! We still get the following output:\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n[02:42:43.194] [SYS] Starting Automation Server on port 9999...\n[02:42:43.647] [SYS] Client Connected: bot_tignb4mrt via HttpPolling\n[02:42:48.667] [TX -\u003E bot_tignb4mrt] EVAL | Code: console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\n[02:42:49.701] [ERR] Illegal return statement\n[02:42:54.370] [TX -\u003E bot_tignb4mrt] EVAL | Code: console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027);\n[02:42:55.534] [RX \u003C- bot_tignb4mrt] Status: completed | Output: undefined\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nKeep in mind that we are running this test on chat.deepseek.com\nHowever, we want to ensure that we cna run js on any website. \nNow the console.log statment is working, however, the return statment does not still.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe \u0022Illegal return statement\u0022 error persists because the target website (like chat.deepseek.com) is likely overriding or polyfilling the \u0060Function\u0060 and \u0060AsyncFunction\u0060 constructors. When these constructors are hooked, they often pass the raw string directly to \u0060eval()\u0060 under the hood. Since the raw string contains a \u0060return\u0060 statement but isn\u0027t syntactically wrapped in a function at the time of evaluation, the JavaScript engine throws a \u0060SyntaxError\u0060.\n\nTo completely bypass any constructor hooks and ensure the \u0060return\u0060 statement is always syntactically valid, we can explicitly wrap the payload in an Immediately Invoked Async Function Expression (IIAFE) and evaluate it directly using the native \u0060eval\u0060 keyword.\n\n### How to test:\n\n- Click **Generate \u0026 Copy Script** in the TestBedApp and update your Tampermonkey script.\n- Reload the target page to connect the updated client.\n- Send the payload \u0060console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\u0060 via the **Test Eval (JS)** button. It will now correctly evaluate the code and return \u0060Success\u0060.\n\n--CODESTART--\n\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\\Generators\\ScriptGenerator.cs**\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n    registerAction(\u0027EVAL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\n        const AsyncFunction = Object.getPrototypeOf(async function(){}).constructor;\n        const func = new AsyncFunction(payload.code);\n        return await func();\n    });\n==== REPLACE\n    registerAction(\u0027EVAL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\n        return await eval(\u0060(async () =\u003E { ${payload.code} })()\u0060);\n    });\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The \u0022Illegal return statement\u0022 error persists because the target website (like chat.deepseek.com) is likely overriding or polyfilling the \u0060Function\u0060 and \u0060AsyncFunction\u0060 constructors. When these constructors are hooked, they often pass the raw string directly to \u0060eval()\u0060 under the hood. Since the raw string contains a \u0060return\u0060 statement but isn\u0027t syntactically wrapped in a function at the time of evaluation, the JavaScript engine throws a \u0060SyntaxError\u0060.\n\nTo completely bypass any constructor hooks and ensure the \u0060return\u0060 statement is always syntactically valid, we can explicitly wrap the payload in an Immediately Invoked Async Function Expression (IIAFE) and evaluate it directly using the native \u0060eval\u0060 keyword.\n\n### How to test:\n\n- Click **Generate \u0026 Copy Script** in the TestBedApp and update your Tampermonkey script.\n- Reload the target page to connect the updated client.\n- Send the payload \u0060console.log(\u0027Hello from C#\u0027); return \u0027Success\u0027;\u0060 via the **Test Eval (JS)** button. It will now correctly evaluate the code and return \u0060Success\u0060.",
