    {
      "Id": "d6bfc583",
      "Timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:59:44.2018477-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "Ok this is great! Now we need to fix a bug on the websocket style of IPC. For some reason, whenver i click the refresh dom button, the websocket disconnects and then reconnects, and as a result, we never get the DOM back for viewing in the test bed app.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nThe issue is caused by the WebSocket server only reading the first 64KB of the incoming message. Since the serialized DOM is often much larger than 64KB, the JSON string gets truncated. When \u0060JsonSerializer.Deserialize\u0060 attempts to parse this incomplete JSON, it throws an exception that crashes the WebSocket connection, causing the client to disconnect and reconnect.\n\nTo fix this, we need to update the WebSocket receive loop to continuously read chunks into a \u0060MemoryStream\u0060 until the \u0060EndOfMessage\u0060 flag is true, ensuring we have the complete payload before attempting to deserialize it.\n\n### How to test:\n\n- Apply the changes to the server code and rebuild/restart the TestBedApp.\n- Connect a client using the WebSocket IPC mode.\n- Click **Refresh DOM**. The server will now correctly assemble the large DOM payload without disconnecting, and the DOM tree will populate in the UI.\n\n--CODESTART--\n\n**C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\\Server\\AutomationServer.cs**\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060cs\n\u003C\u003C\u003C\u003C SEARCH\n            var buffer = new byte[1024 * 64];\n\n            try\n            {\n                while (webSocket.State == WebSocketState.Open \u0026\u0026 !cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested)\n                {\n                    var result = await webSocket.ReceiveAsync(new ArraySegment\u003Cbyte\u003E(buffer), cancellationToken);\n                    if (result.MessageType == WebSocketMessageType.Close)\n                    {\n                        await webSocket.CloseAsync(WebSocketCloseStatus.NormalClosure, \u0022\u0022, cancellationToken);\n                        break;\n                    }\n\n                    string message = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(buffer, 0, result.Count);\n                    var cmdResult = JsonSerializer.Deserialize\u003CCommandResult\u003E(message);\n                    if (cmdResult != null)\n                    {\n                        client.HandleResult(cmdResult);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n==== REPLACE\n            var buffer = new byte[1024 * 64];\n\n            try\n            {\n                while (webSocket.State == WebSocketState.Open \u0026\u0026 !cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested)\n                {\n                    using var ms = new MemoryStream();\n                    WebSocketReceiveResult result;\n                    \n                    do\n                    {\n                        result = await webSocket.ReceiveAsync(new ArraySegment\u003Cbyte\u003E(buffer), cancellationToken);\n                        if (result.MessageType == WebSocketMessageType.Close)\n                        {\n                            await webSocket.CloseAsync(WebSocketCloseStatus.NormalClosure, \u0022\u0022, cancellationToken);\n                            break;\n                        }\n                        ms.Write(buffer, 0, result.Count);\n                    } while (!result.EndOfMessage);\n\n                    if (result.MessageType == WebSocketMessageType.Close)\n                        break;\n\n                    string message = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(ms.ToArray());\n                    var cmdResult = JsonSerializer.Deserialize\u003CCommandResult\u003E(message);\n                    if (cmdResult != null)\n                    {\n                        client.HandleResult(cmdResult);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n\u003E\u003E\u003E\u003E\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
      "ModelExplanation": "The issue is caused by the WebSocket server only reading the first 64KB of the incoming message. Since the serialized DOM is often much larger than 64KB, the JSON string gets truncated. When \u0060JsonSerializer.Deserialize\u0060 attempts to parse this incomplete JSON, it throws an exception that crashes the WebSocket connection, causing the client to disconnect and reconnect.\n\nTo fix this, we need to update the WebSocket receive loop to continuously read chunks into a \u0060MemoryStream\u0060 until the \u0060EndOfMessage\u0060 flag is true, ensuring we have the complete payload before attempting to deserialize it.\n\n### How to test:\n\n- Apply the changes to the server code and rebuild/restart the TestBedApp.\n- Connect a client using the WebSocket IPC mode.\n- Click **Refresh DOM**. The server will now correctly assemble the large DOM payload without disconnecting, and the DOM tree will populate in the UI.",
