      "UserPrompt": "The root directory for this project is: C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\nThe following directories are currently tracked/active for this project (place new files in these directories unless otherwise specified):\n- C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\BrowserAutomationFramework\n- C:\\Users\\Devtop1\\Documents\\GitHub\\baf\\TestBedApp\n\nPlease study this codebase. I want you to document ANY code that is still in the testbed winforms project that isnt strictly UI wrapper code. I want to ensure all the real logic of the browser automation framework is inside the dll. If you find any code in the winforms app that can be migrated to the dll project, please document that. We want to ensure that the winforms app is just a UI wrapper around the dll. This will ensure that other developers can easily work with the framework. Please also ignore all code files in the utils folder in the winforms project, for as those dont need to be migrated at all and can stay where they\u0027re at.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\n### Audit of Non-UI Logic Remaining in \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 (Excluding \u0060utils\u0060)\n\nAfter reviewing \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.cs\u0060, \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.Designer.cs\u0060, \u0060TestBedApp\\Controls\\DomTreeView.cs\u0060, and \u0060TestBedApp\\Program.cs\u0060, the bulk of the core automation, scraping, reverse-engineering, and DOM virtualization engines already reside in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060. However, several pieces of session state management, collection synchronization, workflow orchestration, and layout/theme metadata still live in the WinForms project and can be migrated into \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 so that \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 is strictly a thin UI wrapper.\n\n### 1. \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.cs\u0060\n\n#### A. Redundant \u0060BrowserClient\u0060 Target Resolution (\u0060lstClients.SelectedItem\u0060 vs. \u0060_session.ActiveClient\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BtnSend_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnReactClick_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnEval_Click\u0060\n- \u0060TvDom_AfterSelect\u0060, \u0060MnuClickElement_Click\u0060, \u0060MnuSetValue_Click\u0060, \u0060MnuQuickScrapeText_Click\u0060\n- \u0060BtnRunAutomation_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnHighlightContainer_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnHighlightScrapeField_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnPreviewScrape_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnRunAllScrapes_Click\u0060, \u0060ExecuteSiteDumpFromUiAsync\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- \u0060MainForm\u0060 manually casts \u0060lstClients.SelectedItem as BrowserClient\u0060 and passes it into \u0060_session\u0060 methods, even though \u0060LstClients_SelectedIndexChanged\u0060 and \u0060Server_OnActiveClientChanged\u0060 already keep \u0060_session.ActiveClient\u0060 synchronized with the UI list.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Have \u0060MainForm\u0060 call the parameterless/default-client overloads on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (which already default to \u0060ActiveClient\u0060), eliminating manual client resolution from UI controls across 13 event handlers.\n\n#### B. Dual-Step Collection Reordering \u0026 Index Arithmetic\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BtnMoveUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveDown_Click\u0060 (automation steps)\n- \u0060BtnMoveSeqUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveSeqDown_Click\u0060 (automation sequences)\n- \u0060BtnMoveScrapeUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveScrapeDown_Click\u0060 (scrape definitions)\n- \u0060BtnMoveScrapeFieldUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveScrapeFieldDown_Click\u0060 (scrape fields)\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Each handler performs bounds/index arithmetic (\u0060idx - 1\u0060 or \u0060idx \u002B 1\u0060), calls a model mutation method (e.g., \u0060_session.MoveSequenceUp(idx)\u0060), and then separately calls \u0060_session.MoveItemInList(uiList, idx, idx - 1)\u0060.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Add overloads on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (such as \u0060MoveSequenceUp(IList uiItems, int index, out int newIndex)\u0060, \u0060MoveSequenceDown(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveStepUp(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveStepDown(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveScrapeUp(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveScrapeDown(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveFieldUp(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveFieldDown(...)\u0060) that atomically reorder both the underlying project model list and the bound \u0060IList\u0060 and return the new selected index.\n\n#### C. DOM Context Menu Scrape Creation \u0026 Assignment Workflows\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060CtxDomNode_Opening\u0060, \u0060PopulateScrapeSubMenu\u0060, \u0060AddFieldFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, \u0060SetContainerFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, and \u0060SetPaginationNextFromSelectedDomNode\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- \u0060CtxDomNode_Opening\u0060 manually checks \u0060_session.Project.Sequences.Count == 0\u0060 and \u0060_session.Project.Scrapes.Count \u003E 0\u0060.\n- \u0060PopulateScrapeSubMenu\u0060 contains an inline workflow that prompts for a name using \u0060_session.Project.GetSuggestedScrapeName()\u0060, calls \u0060_session.TryAddScrape(name)\u0060, updates the UI list, and then chains a DOM-node mutation callback (\u0060AddFieldFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, \u0060SetContainerFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, or \u0060SetPaginationNextFromSelectedDomNode\u0060).\n- \u0060AddFieldFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, \u0060SetContainerFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, and \u0060SetPaginationNextFromSelectedDomNode\u0060 also contain branching logic to decide whether to select the target \u0060ScrapeDefinition\u0060 or reload/append fields in the UI.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Expose \u0060HasSequences\u0060 and \u0060HasScrapes\u0060 properties on \u0060AutomationProject\u0060 / \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060.\n- Add helper methods on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 that combine creating a new \u0060ScrapeDefinition\u0060 and immediately applying a \u0060DomNode\u0060 as a field, container, or pagination button in a single session call.\n\n#### D. Reverse-Engineering View State \u0026 Resource Filtering Selection Resolution\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BuildSiteDumpOptionsFromUi\u0060, \u0060GetActiveDumpViewMode\u0060, \u0060RefreshDumpedResourcesList\u0060, \u0060LstDumpedResources_SelectedIndexChanged\u0060, \u0060BtnCopyCurrentDumpView_Click\u0060, and \u0060BtnSaveActiveDumpFile_Click\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Unlike \u0060GetExtensionTabs(filter, preferredSelectedTabId, out resolvedSelectedTab)\u0060 (which resolves the item to select inside the DLL), \u0060RefreshDumpedResourcesList\u0060 calls \u0060_session.FilterLastSiteDumpResources(txtFilterResources.Text)\u0060 and manually decides whether to select index \u00600\u0060 or clear the detail/source text boxes when empty.\n- \u0060MainForm\u0060 also manually tracks the active \u0060SiteDumpViewMode\u0060, \u0060SiteDumpOptions\u0060, and currently selected \u0060SiteResourceItem\u0060, passing them repeatedly into \u0060GetActiveDumpViewTextForCopy\u0060, \u0060TryGetActiveDumpViewForExport\u0060, and \u0060SaveActiveDumpViewToFile\u0060.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Add active reverse-engineering state properties to \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (\u0060DumpOptions\u0060, \u0060ActiveDumpViewMode\u0060, \u0060SelectedDumpResource\u0060, \u0060ResourceFilterText\u0060) and an overload of \u0060FilterLastSiteDumpResources(string? filter, SiteResourceItem? preferredSelection, out SiteResourceItem? resolvedSelection)\u0060 so the session manages resource selection preservation and active view resolution directly.\n\n#### E. DOM Snapshot Refresh State Passing (\u0060ApplyDomSnapshotResultToUi\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060ApplyDomSnapshotResultToUi(BrowserClient client, DomSnapshotRefreshResult result)\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Calls \u0060var expandedPaths = tvDom.GetExpandedPathsSnapshot();\u0060 and passes \u0060expandedPaths\u0060 right back into \u0060tvDom.ApplySnapshot(result.Snapshot, expandedPaths, result.ResolvedSelectedPath, ensureSelectedVisible: result.ScrollSelectionIntoView, raiseAfterSelect: false)\u0060.\n- This round-trip exists because \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplySnapshot\u0060 in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\\Models\\DomTreeSnapshot.cs\u0060 only prunes stale keys from its internal \u0060_expandedKeys\u0060 set when a non-null \u0060previousExpandedKeys\u0060 collection is passed in.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Update \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplySnapshot\u0060 (and \u0060DomTreeSnapshot.PrepareExpandedState\u0060) in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 so that when \u0060previousExpandedKeys\u0060 is \u0060null\u0060, it automatically retains and prunes its own existing \u0060_expandedKeys\u0060 against the new snapshot, and add a \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplyRefreshResult(DomSnapshotRefreshResult result, int viewportWidth, int viewportHeight)\u0060 method so \u0060DomTreeView\u0060 can accept \u0060DomSnapshotRefreshResult\u0060 directly.\n\n#### F. Duplicate \u0060StopServer()\u0060 Call on Server Startup Failure (\u0060BtnStart_Click\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BtnStart_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnStop_Click\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- When \u0060_session.StartServerAsync\u0060 throws a non-cancellation exception (e.g., port in use), \u0060AutomationStudioSession.StartServerAsync\u0060 already catches it, logs the error, and calls \u0060StopServer()\u0060 before rethrowing. \u0060BtnStart_Click\u0060 catches the rethrown exception and calls \u0060BtnStop_Click\u0060, which calls \u0060_session.StopServer()\u0060 a second time (producing a duplicate \u0060\u0022Server stopped.\u0022\u0060 log entry).\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Have \u0060AutomationStudioSession.TryStartServerAsync(int port, IpcMode mode, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)\u0060 return a \u0060bool\u0060 (or raise a \u0060ServerRunningStateChanged\u0060 event) so the WinForms UI only updates button enabled states in response to session state changes rather than duplicating error recovery and server stop calls.\n\n#### G. Proportional Splitter Layout Calculations (\u0060EnsureTabSplittersConfigured\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060EnsureTabSplittersConfigured()\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Contains proportional ratios (\u00600.58\u0060, \u00600.45\u0060, \u00600.28\u0060, \u00600.36\u0060, \u00600.32\u0060, \u00600.52\u0060, \u00600.35\u0060, \u00600.34\u0060, \u00600.65\u0060) and DPI-scaled \u0060Math.Clamp\u0060 bounds calculations for initializing panel sizes across all tabs.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Move the proportional splitter distance clamping helper and studio panel ratio definitions into \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 (e.g., a \u0060StudioLayoutMetrics\u0060 helper or methods on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060), leaving \u0060MainForm\u0060 to simply assign the returned pixel distance to each \u0060SplitContainer.SplitterDistance\u0060.\n\n#### H. Hardcoded Dark Theme Colors \u0026 Status Colors\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060 and \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.Designer.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060UpdateExtensionStatusLabel\u0060 (\u0060Color.LimeGreen\u0060 vs. \u0060Color.Orange\u0060)\n- \u0060InitializeScraperTabUi\u0060, \u0060InitializeReverseEngineeringTabUi\u0060, \u0060InitializeExtensionTabsSidebarUi\u0060, \u0060PromptForValue\u0060 (\u0060Color.FromArgb(30, 30, 30)\u0060, \u0060Color.FromArgb(45, 45, 48)\u0060, \u0060Color.FromArgb(9, 71, 113)\u0060, \u0060Color.FromArgb(127, 127, 127)\u0060)\n- \u0060MainForm.Designer.cs\u0060 (\u0060Text = \u0022Browser Automation Framework - Testbed\u0022\u0060 and \u0060cmbMode.SelectedIndex = 1\u0060 in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060)\n- **What it does**:\n\n- While \u0060DomTokenColorExtensions\u0060 and \u0060AutomationLogLevelExtensions\u0060 in the DLL already centralize dark-theme RGB tuples for the DOM tree and log output, \u0060MainForm\u0060 still hardcodes RGB tuples for extension status colors, grid/surface colors, default \u0060IpcMode\u0060 (\u0060IpcMode.WebSocket\u0060), and the window title.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Add \u0060DefaultIpcMode = IpcMode.WebSocket\u0060 and \u0060GetExtensionStatusColorRgb()\u0060 to \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (and shared dark studio surface RGB constants to \u0060DomTokenColorExtensions\u0060 or a studio theme model in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060), and bind \u0060MainForm.Text\u0060 to \u0060AutomationStudioSession.StudioTitle\u0060.\n\n### 2. \u0060TestBedApp\\Controls\\DomTreeView.cs\u0060\n\n- **Snapshot Refresh Result Application (\u0060ApplySnapshot\u0060)**:\n\n- As noted in **1.E**, \u0060DomTreeView\u0060 can expose a single \u0060ApplySnapshotResult(DomSnapshotRefreshResult result)\u0060 method backed by \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplyRefreshResult(...)\u0060 in the DLL so \u0060MainForm\u0060 does not have to manually snapshot and pass back expanded path keys.\n- **Unconditional \u0060AfterSelect\u0060 Firing on Mouse Down (\u0060OnMouseDown\u0060)**:\n\n- In \u0060OnMouseDown\u0060, \u0060_state.HandlePointerDown(...)\u0060 computes \u0060out bool selectionChanged\u0060, but \u0060DomTreeView\u0060 discards it (\u0060out _\u0060) and invokes \u0060RaiseAfterSelect(hitItem.Node, TreeViewAction.ByMouse)\u0060 even when clicking an already-selected row or right-clicking. Using \u0060selectionChanged\u0060 (or letting \u0060DomTreeViewportState\u0060 determine whether selection notification should fire) avoids redundant \u0060AfterSelect\u0060 notifications.\n- **Redundant Scrollbar Layout Calculation in \u0060OnPaint\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060OnPaint\u0060 calls \u0060_state.CalculateScrollBarLayout(...)\u0060 on every paint frame solely to obtain \u0060cornerRect\u0060. \u0060DomTreeViewportState\u0060 can cache or expose \u0060GetScrollCornerBounds(...)\u0060 directly.\n\n### 3. \u0060TestBedApp\\Program.cs\u0060\n\n- \u0060Program.cs\u0060 contains only the standard \u0060[STAThread] static void Main(string[] args)\u0060 WinForms initialization (\u0060ApplicationConfiguration.Initialize()\u0060, \u0060Application.SetColorMode(SystemColorMode.Dark)\u0060, \u0060Application.SetHighDpiMode(HighDpiMode.PerMonitorV2)\u0060, and \u0060Application.Run(new MainForm(args))\u0060). No non-UI logic exists in \u0060Program.cs\u0060.",
      "ModelExplanation": "### Audit of Non-UI Logic Remaining in \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 (Excluding \u0060utils\u0060)\n\nAfter reviewing \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.cs\u0060, \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.Designer.cs\u0060, \u0060TestBedApp\\Controls\\DomTreeView.cs\u0060, and \u0060TestBedApp\\Program.cs\u0060, the bulk of the core automation, scraping, reverse-engineering, and DOM virtualization engines already reside in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060. However, several pieces of session state management, collection synchronization, workflow orchestration, and layout/theme metadata still live in the WinForms project and can be migrated into \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 so that \u0060TestBedApp\u0060 is strictly a thin UI wrapper.\n\n### 1. \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.cs\u0060\n\n#### A. Redundant \u0060BrowserClient\u0060 Target Resolution (\u0060lstClients.SelectedItem\u0060 vs. \u0060_session.ActiveClient\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BtnSend_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnReactClick_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnEval_Click\u0060\n- \u0060TvDom_AfterSelect\u0060, \u0060MnuClickElement_Click\u0060, \u0060MnuSetValue_Click\u0060, \u0060MnuQuickScrapeText_Click\u0060\n- \u0060BtnRunAutomation_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnHighlightContainer_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnHighlightScrapeField_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnPreviewScrape_Click\u0060, \u0060BtnRunAllScrapes_Click\u0060, \u0060ExecuteSiteDumpFromUiAsync\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- \u0060MainForm\u0060 manually casts \u0060lstClients.SelectedItem as BrowserClient\u0060 and passes it into \u0060_session\u0060 methods, even though \u0060LstClients_SelectedIndexChanged\u0060 and \u0060Server_OnActiveClientChanged\u0060 already keep \u0060_session.ActiveClient\u0060 synchronized with the UI list.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Have \u0060MainForm\u0060 call the parameterless/default-client overloads on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (which already default to \u0060ActiveClient\u0060), eliminating manual client resolution from UI controls across 13 event handlers.\n\n#### B. Dual-Step Collection Reordering \u0026 Index Arithmetic\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BtnMoveUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveDown_Click\u0060 (automation steps)\n- \u0060BtnMoveSeqUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveSeqDown_Click\u0060 (automation sequences)\n- \u0060BtnMoveScrapeUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveScrapeDown_Click\u0060 (scrape definitions)\n- \u0060BtnMoveScrapeFieldUp_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnMoveScrapeFieldDown_Click\u0060 (scrape fields)\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Each handler performs bounds/index arithmetic (\u0060idx - 1\u0060 or \u0060idx \u002B 1\u0060), calls a model mutation method (e.g., \u0060_session.MoveSequenceUp(idx)\u0060), and then separately calls \u0060_session.MoveItemInList(uiList, idx, idx - 1)\u0060.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Add overloads on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (such as \u0060MoveSequenceUp(IList uiItems, int index, out int newIndex)\u0060, \u0060MoveSequenceDown(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveStepUp(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveStepDown(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveScrapeUp(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveScrapeDown(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveFieldUp(...)\u0060, \u0060MoveFieldDown(...)\u0060) that atomically reorder both the underlying project model list and the bound \u0060IList\u0060 and return the new selected index.\n\n#### C. DOM Context Menu Scrape Creation \u0026 Assignment Workflows\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060CtxDomNode_Opening\u0060, \u0060PopulateScrapeSubMenu\u0060, \u0060AddFieldFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, \u0060SetContainerFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, and \u0060SetPaginationNextFromSelectedDomNode\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- \u0060CtxDomNode_Opening\u0060 manually checks \u0060_session.Project.Sequences.Count == 0\u0060 and \u0060_session.Project.Scrapes.Count \u003E 0\u0060.\n- \u0060PopulateScrapeSubMenu\u0060 contains an inline workflow that prompts for a name using \u0060_session.Project.GetSuggestedScrapeName()\u0060, calls \u0060_session.TryAddScrape(name)\u0060, updates the UI list, and then chains a DOM-node mutation callback (\u0060AddFieldFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, \u0060SetContainerFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, or \u0060SetPaginationNextFromSelectedDomNode\u0060).\n- \u0060AddFieldFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, \u0060SetContainerFromSelectedDomNode\u0060, and \u0060SetPaginationNextFromSelectedDomNode\u0060 also contain branching logic to decide whether to select the target \u0060ScrapeDefinition\u0060 or reload/append fields in the UI.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Expose \u0060HasSequences\u0060 and \u0060HasScrapes\u0060 properties on \u0060AutomationProject\u0060 / \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060.\n- Add helper methods on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 that combine creating a new \u0060ScrapeDefinition\u0060 and immediately applying a \u0060DomNode\u0060 as a field, container, or pagination button in a single session call.\n\n#### D. Reverse-Engineering View State \u0026 Resource Filtering Selection Resolution\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BuildSiteDumpOptionsFromUi\u0060, \u0060GetActiveDumpViewMode\u0060, \u0060RefreshDumpedResourcesList\u0060, \u0060LstDumpedResources_SelectedIndexChanged\u0060, \u0060BtnCopyCurrentDumpView_Click\u0060, and \u0060BtnSaveActiveDumpFile_Click\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Unlike \u0060GetExtensionTabs(filter, preferredSelectedTabId, out resolvedSelectedTab)\u0060 (which resolves the item to select inside the DLL), \u0060RefreshDumpedResourcesList\u0060 calls \u0060_session.FilterLastSiteDumpResources(txtFilterResources.Text)\u0060 and manually decides whether to select index \u00600\u0060 or clear the detail/source text boxes when empty.\n- \u0060MainForm\u0060 also manually tracks the active \u0060SiteDumpViewMode\u0060, \u0060SiteDumpOptions\u0060, and currently selected \u0060SiteResourceItem\u0060, passing them repeatedly into \u0060GetActiveDumpViewTextForCopy\u0060, \u0060TryGetActiveDumpViewForExport\u0060, and \u0060SaveActiveDumpViewToFile\u0060.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Add active reverse-engineering state properties to \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (\u0060DumpOptions\u0060, \u0060ActiveDumpViewMode\u0060, \u0060SelectedDumpResource\u0060, \u0060ResourceFilterText\u0060) and an overload of \u0060FilterLastSiteDumpResources(string? filter, SiteResourceItem? preferredSelection, out SiteResourceItem? resolvedSelection)\u0060 so the session manages resource selection preservation and active view resolution directly.\n\n#### E. DOM Snapshot Refresh State Passing (\u0060ApplyDomSnapshotResultToUi\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060ApplyDomSnapshotResultToUi(BrowserClient client, DomSnapshotRefreshResult result)\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Calls \u0060var expandedPaths = tvDom.GetExpandedPathsSnapshot();\u0060 and passes \u0060expandedPaths\u0060 right back into \u0060tvDom.ApplySnapshot(result.Snapshot, expandedPaths, result.ResolvedSelectedPath, ensureSelectedVisible: result.ScrollSelectionIntoView, raiseAfterSelect: false)\u0060.\n- This round-trip exists because \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplySnapshot\u0060 in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\\Models\\DomTreeSnapshot.cs\u0060 only prunes stale keys from its internal \u0060_expandedKeys\u0060 set when a non-null \u0060previousExpandedKeys\u0060 collection is passed in.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Update \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplySnapshot\u0060 (and \u0060DomTreeSnapshot.PrepareExpandedState\u0060) in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 so that when \u0060previousExpandedKeys\u0060 is \u0060null\u0060, it automatically retains and prunes its own existing \u0060_expandedKeys\u0060 against the new snapshot, and add a \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplyRefreshResult(DomSnapshotRefreshResult result, int viewportWidth, int viewportHeight)\u0060 method so \u0060DomTreeView\u0060 can accept \u0060DomSnapshotRefreshResult\u0060 directly.\n\n#### F. Duplicate \u0060StopServer()\u0060 Call on Server Startup Failure (\u0060BtnStart_Click\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060BtnStart_Click\u0060 and \u0060BtnStop_Click\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- When \u0060_session.StartServerAsync\u0060 throws a non-cancellation exception (e.g., port in use), \u0060AutomationStudioSession.StartServerAsync\u0060 already catches it, logs the error, and calls \u0060StopServer()\u0060 before rethrowing. \u0060BtnStart_Click\u0060 catches the rethrown exception and calls \u0060BtnStop_Click\u0060, which calls \u0060_session.StopServer()\u0060 a second time (producing a duplicate \u0060\u0022Server stopped.\u0022\u0060 log entry).\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Have \u0060AutomationStudioSession.TryStartServerAsync(int port, IpcMode mode, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)\u0060 return a \u0060bool\u0060 (or raise a \u0060ServerRunningStateChanged\u0060 event) so the WinForms UI only updates button enabled states in response to session state changes rather than duplicating error recovery and server stop calls.\n\n#### G. Proportional Splitter Layout Calculations (\u0060EnsureTabSplittersConfigured\u0060)\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060EnsureTabSplittersConfigured()\u0060\n- **What it does**:\n\n- Contains proportional ratios (\u00600.58\u0060, \u00600.45\u0060, \u00600.28\u0060, \u00600.36\u0060, \u00600.32\u0060, \u00600.52\u0060, \u00600.35\u0060, \u00600.34\u0060, \u00600.65\u0060) and DPI-scaled \u0060Math.Clamp\u0060 bounds calculations for initializing panel sizes across all tabs.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Move the proportional splitter distance clamping helper and studio panel ratio definitions into \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 (e.g., a \u0060StudioLayoutMetrics\u0060 helper or methods on \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060), leaving \u0060MainForm\u0060 to simply assign the returned pixel distance to each \u0060SplitContainer.SplitterDistance\u0060.\n\n#### H. Hardcoded Dark Theme Colors \u0026 Status Colors\n\n- **Where in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060 and \u0060TestBedApp\\MainForm.Designer.cs\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060UpdateExtensionStatusLabel\u0060 (\u0060Color.LimeGreen\u0060 vs. \u0060Color.Orange\u0060)\n- \u0060InitializeScraperTabUi\u0060, \u0060InitializeReverseEngineeringTabUi\u0060, \u0060InitializeExtensionTabsSidebarUi\u0060, \u0060PromptForValue\u0060 (\u0060Color.FromArgb(30, 30, 30)\u0060, \u0060Color.FromArgb(45, 45, 48)\u0060, \u0060Color.FromArgb(9, 71, 113)\u0060, \u0060Color.FromArgb(127, 127, 127)\u0060)\n- \u0060MainForm.Designer.cs\u0060 (\u0060Text = \u0022Browser Automation Framework - Testbed\u0022\u0060 and \u0060cmbMode.SelectedIndex = 1\u0060 in \u0060MainForm.cs\u0060)\n- **What it does**:\n\n- While \u0060DomTokenColorExtensions\u0060 and \u0060AutomationLogLevelExtensions\u0060 in the DLL already centralize dark-theme RGB tuples for the DOM tree and log output, \u0060MainForm\u0060 still hardcodes RGB tuples for extension status colors, grid/surface colors, default \u0060IpcMode\u0060 (\u0060IpcMode.WebSocket\u0060), and the window title.\n- **How to migrate to the DLL**:\n\n- Add \u0060DefaultIpcMode = IpcMode.WebSocket\u0060 and \u0060GetExtensionStatusColorRgb()\u0060 to \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060 (and shared dark studio surface RGB constants to \u0060DomTokenColorExtensions\u0060 or a studio theme model in \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060), and bind \u0060MainForm.Text\u0060 to \u0060AutomationStudioSession.StudioTitle\u0060.\n\n### 2. \u0060TestBedApp\\Controls\\DomTreeView.cs\u0060\n\n- **Snapshot Refresh Result Application (\u0060ApplySnapshot\u0060)**:\n\n- As noted in **1.E**, \u0060DomTreeView\u0060 can expose a single \u0060ApplySnapshotResult(DomSnapshotRefreshResult result)\u0060 method backed by \u0060DomTreeViewportState.ApplyRefreshResult(...)\u0060 in the DLL so \u0060MainForm\u0060 does not have to manually snapshot and pass back expanded path keys.\n- **Unconditional \u0060AfterSelect\u0060 Firing on Mouse Down (\u0060OnMouseDown\u0060)**:\n\n- In \u0060OnMouseDown\u0060, \u0060_state.HandlePointerDown(...)\u0060 computes \u0060out bool selectionChanged\u0060, but \u0060DomTreeView\u0060 discards it (\u0060out _\u0060) and invokes \u0060RaiseAfterSelect(hitItem.Node, TreeViewAction.ByMouse)\u0060 even when clicking an already-selected row or right-clicking. Using \u0060selectionChanged\u0060 (or letting \u0060DomTreeViewportState\u0060 determine whether selection notification should fire) avoids redundant \u0060AfterSelect\u0060 notifications.\n- **Redundant Scrollbar Layout Calculation in \u0060OnPaint\u0060**:\n\n- \u0060OnPaint\u0060 calls \u0060_state.CalculateScrollBarLayout(...)\u0060 on every paint frame solely to obtain \u0060cornerRect\u0060. \u0060DomTreeViewportState\u0060 can cache or expose \u0060GetScrollCornerBounds(...)\u0060 directly.\n\n### 3. \u0060TestBedApp\\Program.cs\u0060\n\n- \u0060Program.cs\u0060 contains only the standard \u0060[STAThread] static void Main(string[] args)\u0060 WinForms initialization (\u0060ApplicationConfiguration.Initialize()\u0060, \u0060Application.SetColorMode(SystemColorMode.Dark)\u0060, \u0060Application.SetHighDpiMode(HighDpiMode.PerMonitorV2)\u0060, and \u0060Application.Run(new MainForm(args))\u0060). No non-UI logic exists in \u0060Program.cs\u0060.",
