      "UserPrompt": "This is perfect! Just edit the end of the article to link both of the following links for the repos for this project (so people can find the project)\nhttps://git.churchofmalware.org/mastercodeon/baf\nhttps://github.com/codeon314/baf",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nUpdated the closing section of the article to include both repository links (\u0060https://git.churchofmalware.org/mastercodeon/baf\u0060 and \u0060https://github.com/codeon314/baf\u0060) so readers can easily find and clone the project. No project files were modified.\n\n# Stop Fighting CDP Detection: Automating Your Actual Browser with C# and BAF\n\nIf you have spent any time writing web scrapers or browser automations over the last couple of years, you already know how the cat-and-mouse game goes. You start with Playwright or Puppeteer. Everything works great on a local test page. Then you point it at a real production site behind Cloudflare Turnstile, DataDome, or Akamai, and you immediately hit a wall.\n\nSo you reach for \u0060puppeteer-extra-plugin-stealth\u0060, or maybe you switch to Patchright, or you start passing twenty different Chromium launch flags to hide \u0060navigator.webdriver\u0060. Sometimes that buys you a week. Eventually, something else gives you away: a subtle Chrome DevTools Protocol (\u0060Runtime.enable\u0060) leak, a sterile browser profile with zero browsing history, a mismatch in your JA3/JA4 TLS fingerprint, or the simple fact that an automation binary is spawning a child Chromium process with a debugging pipe attached.\n\nWhile building **Browser Automation Framework (BAF)**\u2014a .NET 10 library and companion desktop IDE called **BAF Studio**\u2014the goal was to step off that treadmill entirely. Instead of trying to make a fake browser look human, BAF just uses the browser you already have open.\n\n## Flipping the Architecture Inside Out\n\nEvery mainstream automation tool works from the outside in: your code launches a browser binary and drives it over WebDriver or the Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP).\n\nBAF flips that relationship. You keep using your normal, everyday browser\u2014the one with your actual GPU state, extensions, cookies, active logins, and organic trust score. BAF spins up a lightweight local HTTP/WebSocket server in C# (\u0060AutomationServer\u0060 on \u0060127.0.0.1\u0060) and talks to a small JavaScript bridge running directly inside the page.\n\nThere are two ways that bridge gets into the browser:\n\n- **An Unpacked Manifest V3 Chrome Extension (Recommended for Chromium browsers):** BAF generates the extension files (\u0060manifest.json\u0060, \u0060background.js\u0060, \u0060content_relay.js\u0060, \u0060bridge.js\u0060, \u0060popup.html\u0060, and \u0060popup.js\u0060) right out of the box and keeps them synced with your server port. Once loaded into Chrome, Edge, or Brave, the background worker reports every open tab back to your C# app in real time. You can inject the bridge into a tab with one click, switch tabs exclusively, or detach cleanly when you are done.\n- **A Tampermonkey Userscript (Required for Firefox):** Because BAF doesn\u0027t have a native Firefox extension yet, Firefox users (or anyone who prefers userscripts) can generate a ready-to-paste Tampermonkey script from C# or BAF Studio.\n\nBecause no external debugger is attached to the browser, anti-bot scripts looking for CDP artifacts come up empty. To the target site, network requests and TLS handshakes are coming from your everyday browser\u2014because they are.\n\n## Surviving Strict CSP Rules and Rate Limits\n\nRunning inside the page context introduces two classic headaches: Content-Security-Policy (\u0060connect-src\u0060) rules blocking local WebSockets, and full-page navigations wiping out your script state.\n\n### Dual-Transport IPC (WebSocket \u002B HTTP Polling Fallback)\n\nBy default, BAF communicates over full-duplex WebSockets (\u0060ws://127.0.0.1:{port}\u0060) for low-latency command execution, DOM mutation events, and streaming data.\n\nHowever, strict sites (like GitHub, banking portals, or heavily locked-down SPAs) often set CSP headers that forbid connecting to \u0060ws://127.0.0.1\u0060. When that happens, BAF\u0027s bridge catches the \u0060securitypolicyviolation\u0060 event (or detects the \u0060\u003Cmeta\u003E\u0060 policy ahead of time) and automatically drops down to HTTP polling via \u0060GM_xmlhttpRequest\u0060.\n\nIn the Chrome Extension, \u0060content_relay.js\u0060 runs in the \u0060ISOLATED\u0060 world and relays those requests to the background service worker, which has host permissions to talk to \u0060127.0.0.1\u0060 regardless of the page\u0027s CSP. Your C# code doesn\u0027t have to change a single line\u2014\u0060BrowserClient\u0060 switches its \u0060Mode\u0060 property from \u0060IpcMode.WebSocket\u0060 to \u0060IpcMode.HttpPolling\u0060 on the fly and keeps running pending commands.\n\n### In-Place Navigation and HTTP 429 Recovery\n\nWhen you navigate to a same-origin page or click a standard link, BAF\u0027s \u0060safeNavigate\u0060 function intercepts the transition, fetches the HTML with your current credentials, swaps the DOM in place, and updates \u0060window.history\u0060.\n\nThis does two things:\n\n- It keeps the WebSocket connection alive across page turns.\n- It lets BAF inspect the raw HTTP status code and headers before the browser renders the page.\n\nIf a site throws an **HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests)** during a multi-page scrape, BAF doesn\u0027t crash your run. The bridge displays a small status banner at the top of the tab, extracts the \u0060Retry-After\u0060, \u0060X-Retry-After\u0060, or \u0060RateLimit-Reset\u0060 header (whether it\u0027s in seconds, a Unix epoch, or an HTTP-date timestamp), and reports it to \u0060DataScraper\u0060 in C#. The scraper pauses until the cooldown expires, bumps up \u0060PageDelayMs\u0060 for future pages so you stop tripping the limiter, and retries the page automatically.\n\n## Dealing with React Without Losing Your Mind\n\nAnyone who has tried to automate a modern React form with \u0060document.querySelector(\u0027input\u0027).value = \u0027foo\u0027\u0060 knows that React completely ignores it. React overrides the native \u0060value\u0060 setter on the DOM node to track state internally; if you don\u0027t bypass that tracker before dispatching an \u0060input\u0060 event, the component\u0027s state resets on the next render.\n\nBAF\u0027s \u0060ReactExtensions\u0060 handle this in the bridge:\n\n- **\u0060SetReactInputValueAsync\u0060**: Grabs the native \u0060HTMLInputElement.prototype\u0060 value setter directly, applies the value (or uses \u0060document.execCommand(\u0027insertText\u0027)\u0060), and fires bubbling \u0060input\u0060 and \u0060change\u0060 events.\n- **\u0060ClickReactElementAsync\u0060**: Looks for React\u0027s internal \u0060__reactProps$\u0060 or \u0060__reactEventHandlers$\u0060 keys on the element. If it finds an \u0060onClick\u0060 handler, it invokes it with a \u0060Proxy\u0060 around the native \u0060MouseEvent\u0060 that reports \u0060isTrusted: true\u0060. If it\u0027s a standard element or SPA link, it dispatches a full \u0060pointerdown\u0060 -\u003E \u0060mousedown\u0060 -\u003E \u0060pointerup\u0060 -\u003E \u0060mouseup\u0060 -\u003E \u0060click\u0060 sequence.\n\nWriting this in C# looks like standard async code:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\nusing BrowserAutomationFramework.Extensions;\nusing BrowserAutomationFramework.Models;\nusing BrowserAutomationFramework.Server;\n\nusing var server = new AutomationServer(port: 9999);\nserver.SyncDefaultChromeExtension(IpcMode.WebSocket);\n\nserver.OnClientConnected \u002B= async (sender, client) =\u003E\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\u0022Hooked into: {client.DisplayName}\u0022);\n\n    await client.NavigateToUrlAsync(\u0022https://example.com/login\u0022);\n    await client.SetReactInputValueAsync(\u0022#email\u0022, \u0022user@example.com\u0022, overwrite: true);\n    await client.SetReactInputValueAsync(\u0022#password\u0022, \u0022hunter2\u0022, overwrite: true);\n    await client.ClickReactElementAsync(\u0022button[type=\u0027submit\u0027]\u0022);\n\n    var title = await client.EvaluateJavaScriptAsync(\u0022return document.title;\u0022);\n    Console.WriteLine($\u0022Landed on: {title.Output}\u0022);\n};\n\nawait server.StartAsync();\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\n## Structured Scraping: From Point-and-Click to POCOs\n\nSometimes you just want to pull a quick table (\u0060await client.ScrapeTableAsync(\u0022table.results\u0022)\u0060), and other times you need to crawl fifty pages of product cards with pre-scrape login sequences and regex cleanup.\n\nBAF\u0027s scraping engine (\u0060DataScraper\u0060 and the fluent \u0060ScrapeBuilder\u0060) supports 12 extraction types (\u0060InnerText\u0060, \u0060TextContent\u0060, \u0060InnerHtml\u0060, \u0060OuterHtml\u0060, \u0060Value\u0060, \u0060Attribute\u0060, \u0060Href\u0060, \u0060Src\u0060, \u0060DataAttribute\u0060, \u0060ComputedStyle\u0060, \u0060Exists\u0060, and \u0060Count\u0060).\n\nIn code, you can chain a multi-page scrape together and bind the output directly to a C# class:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\npublic class PartListing\n{\n    public string Sku { get; set; } = \u0022\u0022;\n    public decimal Price { get; set; }\n    public string ProductUrl { get; set; } = \u0022\u0022;\n}\n\nScrapeResult result = await client.CreateScrape(\u0022PartsCatalog\u0022)\n    .WithContainer(\u0022div.part-row\u0022)\n    .Field(\u0022Sku\u0022, \u0022span.sku-code\u0022, ScrapeExtractionType.InnerText)\n    .Field(\u0022Price\u0022, \u0022span.price\u0022, ScrapeExtractionType.InnerText, regexPattern: @\u0022([0-9]\u002B\\.[0-9]{2})\u0022)\n    .Field(\u0022ProductUrl\u0022, \u0022a.part-link\u0022, ScrapeExtractionType.Href)\n    .WithPagination(nextPageSelector: \u0022a.pagination-next\u0022, maxPages: 10, pageDelayMs: 1500, deduplicateRows: true)\n    .ExecuteAsync();\n\nList\u003CPartListing\u003E parts = result.BindTo\u003CPartListing\u003E();\nawait result.SaveToCsvFileAsync(\u0022parts.csv\u0022);\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nIf a site doesn\u0027t have a \u0022Next\u0022 button, you can swap \u0060.WithPagination(...)\u0060 for \u0060.WithUrlPagination(\u0022https://example.com/parts?page={page}\u0022)\u0060 or \u0060.WithPaginationSequence(customScrollSequence)\u0060 to run an \u0060AutomationSequence\u0060 between pages.\n\n## Reverse-Engineering Live Sites with \u0060SiteDumper\u0060\n\nModern web apps love to split their styles and logic across external bundles, inline \u0060\u003Cstyle\u003E\u0060 tags, CSSOM constructed stylesheets, \u0060document.adoptedStyleSheets\u0060, and dynamically fetched JS chunks that don\u0027t even appear in the initial HTML source.\n\nBAF includes a dedicated \u0060SiteDumper\u0060 engine that pulls all of these out of the live page:\n\n- **HTML (\u0060DumpHtmlAsync\u0060):** Captures the live document HTML (automatically stripping out BAF\u0027s own highlight overlays and temporary script tags) plus any accessible same-origin or cross-origin \u0060\u003Ciframe\u003E\u0060 documents.\n- **CSS (\u0060DumpCssAsync\u0060):** Fetches external \u0060\u003Clink rel=\u0022stylesheet\u0022\u003E\u0060 files, grabs inline \u0060\u003Cstyle\u003E\u0060 blocks, and serializes runtime CSSOM rules from constructed and adopted stylesheets.\n- **JavaScript (\u0060DumpJavaScriptAsync\u0060):** Collects external \u0060\u003Cscript src\u003E\u0060 files, inline script/JSON blocks, and queries \u0060performance.getEntriesByType(\u0027resource\u0027)\u0060 to catch scripts that were lazy-loaded after page startup.\n\nCalling \u0060await dump.SaveToDirectoryAsync(@\u0022C:\\SiteDump\u0022)\u0060 writes out a clean folder structure (\u0060index.html\u0060, \u0060css/\u0060, \u0060js/\u0060, \u0060combined.css\u0060, \u0060combined.js\u0060, and a full \u0060manifest.json\u0060 inventory).\n\n## BAF Studio: The Visual IDE Built on the Same Core\n\nWriting selectors by hand from DevTools gets old fast. That\u0027s why the repository includes **BAF Studio**, a dark-mode WinForms IDE built on top of \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework.dll\u0060.\n\nNotably, **100% of the logic\u2014even the layout math and virtualized tree state\u2014lives in the UI-agnostic \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 class library.** BAF Studio is just a thin desktop front-end over \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060.\n\nHere is what BAF Studio gives you out of the box:\n\n### 1. Virtualized 60fps DOM Explorer \u0026 Middle-Click Picker\n\nStandard Win32 \u0060TreeView\u0060 controls choke when you feed them a 15,000-node DOM tree. BAF builds an indexed, syntax-tokenized \u0060DomTreeSnapshot\u0060 on a background thread and renders only the visible rows inside a custom virtualized control (\u0060DomTreeView\u0060).\n\n- Clicking a node in BAF Studio highlights it in your browser.\n- **Middle-clicking any element in your browser** highlights it on the page and immediately expands and scrolls to that exact node in BAF Studio\u0027s DOM tree.\n\n### 2. Automatic Selector Generalization\n\nWhen you right-click a DOM node in BAF Studio, you don\u0027t just get its raw \u0060:nth-child()\u0060 path. Using \u0060SelectorHelper\u0060, you can:\n\n- **Set as Repeating Container:** Automatically generalizes the container path (e.g., turning a specific card into a selector that matches all cards on the page) and converts your existing field selectors into \u0060:scope\u0060-relative paths.\n- **Add Repeating Field (All Similar):** Walks up the DOM snapshot to find the repeating row or structural wrapper and strips \u0060:nth-child(N)\u0060 from the right segment so a single field extracts an entire column of data.\n- **Set as Pagination Next Button:** Resolves inner \u0060\u003Cspan\u003E\u0060 or icon clicks up to the parent \u0060\u003Ca\u003E\u0060 or \u0060\u003Cbutton\u003E\u0060 and prefers resilient attributes like \u0060rel=\u0022next\u0022\u0060, \u0060aria-label\u0060, or \u0060id\u0060.\n\n### 3. Workflow \u0026 Reverse-Engineering Tabs\n\nFrom the same window, you can build multi-step \u0060AutomationSequence\u0060 lists, hook them up as Pre-Scrape or Post-Scrape actions, preview scrape results in a live table or JSON view, and run one-click HTML/CSS/JS site dumps with instant code search. Everything saves to a single, portable \u0060.json\u0060 project file that you can either run in the GUI or load headlessly in C# via \u0060AutomationProject.LoadFromFile(...)\u0060.\n\n## Extending the Bridge with Custom JS \u0026 Streaming\n\nIf the built-in actions (\u0060Click\u0060, \u0060SetValue\u0060, \u0060Navigate\u0060, \u0060EvaluateScript\u0060, \u0060Scrape\u0060, \u0060DumpSite\u0060) aren\u0027t enough, you can inject your own JavaScript handlers into the bridge via \u0060ScriptOptions.CustomScript\u0060 and stream intermediate results back to C# as they happen:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060javascript\nregisterAction(\u0027INFINITE_SCROLL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\n    for (let step = 1; step \u003C= payload.maxSteps; step\u002B\u002B) {\n        window.scrollTo(0, document.body.scrollHeight);\n        await new Promise(r =\u003E setTimeout(r, 800));\n        // Stream progress back to BrowserClient.OnStreamDataReceived\n        await window.streamData(payload.taskId, { step, height: document.body.scrollHeight });\n    }\n    return \u0027Scroll finished\u0027;\n});\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nOn the C# side, you can register custom step handlers on \u0060AutomationRunner\u0060 using \u0060runner.RegisterCustomActionHandler(...)\u0060 so your custom logic plugs right into saved project sequences.\n\n## Wrapping Up \u0026 Where to Get It\n\nPlaywright and Selenium are still great for CI/CD testing where you want a clean, disposable browser environment on a build server.\n\nBut when you are scraping rate-limited data, automating workflows inside authenticated sessions, reverse-engineering a complex front-end, or dealing with aggressive bot protection that flags headless and CDP-driven browsers on sight, automating your real browser session is a much calmer way to work.\n\nTo try it out, clone the repo, run \u0060dotnet run --project \u0022BAF Studio/BAF Studio.csproj\u0022\u0060, load the auto-synced Chrome Extension folder from \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework/ChromeExtension\u0060 (or grab the Tampermonkey script for Firefox), and middle-click an element on any open tab.\n\nYou can find the source code, full API reference, and BAF Studio at either of the project repositories:\n\n- **Church of Malware Git:** [https://git.churchofmalware.org/mastercodeon/baf](https://www.google.com/url?sa=E\u0026q=https%3A%2F%2Fgit.churchofmalware.org%2Fmastercodeon%2Fbaf)\n- **GitHub:** [https://github.com/codeon314/baf](https://www.google.com/url?sa=E\u0026q=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcodeon314%2Fbaf)",
      "ModelExplanation": "Updated the closing section of the article to include both repository links (\u0060https://git.churchofmalware.org/mastercodeon/baf\u0060 and \u0060https://github.com/codeon314/baf\u0060) so readers can easily find and clone the project. No project files were modified.\n\n# Stop Fighting CDP Detection: Automating Your Actual Browser with C# and BAF\n\nIf you have spent any time writing web scrapers or browser automations over the last couple of years, you already know how the cat-and-mouse game goes. You start with Playwright or Puppeteer. Everything works great on a local test page. Then you point it at a real production site behind Cloudflare Turnstile, DataDome, or Akamai, and you immediately hit a wall.\n\nSo you reach for \u0060puppeteer-extra-plugin-stealth\u0060, or maybe you switch to Patchright, or you start passing twenty different Chromium launch flags to hide \u0060navigator.webdriver\u0060. Sometimes that buys you a week. Eventually, something else gives you away: a subtle Chrome DevTools Protocol (\u0060Runtime.enable\u0060) leak, a sterile browser profile with zero browsing history, a mismatch in your JA3/JA4 TLS fingerprint, or the simple fact that an automation binary is spawning a child Chromium process with a debugging pipe attached.\n\nWhile building **Browser Automation Framework (BAF)**\u2014a .NET 10 library and companion desktop IDE called **BAF Studio**\u2014the goal was to step off that treadmill entirely. Instead of trying to make a fake browser look human, BAF just uses the browser you already have open.\n\n## Flipping the Architecture Inside Out\n\nEvery mainstream automation tool works from the outside in: your code launches a browser binary and drives it over WebDriver or the Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP).\n\nBAF flips that relationship. You keep using your normal, everyday browser\u2014the one with your actual GPU state, extensions, cookies, active logins, and organic trust score. BAF spins up a lightweight local HTTP/WebSocket server in C# (\u0060AutomationServer\u0060 on \u0060127.0.0.1\u0060) and talks to a small JavaScript bridge running directly inside the page.\n\nThere are two ways that bridge gets into the browser:\n\n- **An Unpacked Manifest V3 Chrome Extension (Recommended for Chromium browsers):** BAF generates the extension files (\u0060manifest.json\u0060, \u0060background.js\u0060, \u0060content_relay.js\u0060, \u0060bridge.js\u0060, \u0060popup.html\u0060, and \u0060popup.js\u0060) right out of the box and keeps them synced with your server port. Once loaded into Chrome, Edge, or Brave, the background worker reports every open tab back to your C# app in real time. You can inject the bridge into a tab with one click, switch tabs exclusively, or detach cleanly when you are done.\n- **A Tampermonkey Userscript (Required for Firefox):** Because BAF doesn\u0027t have a native Firefox extension yet, Firefox users (or anyone who prefers userscripts) can generate a ready-to-paste Tampermonkey script from C# or BAF Studio.\n\nBecause no external debugger is attached to the browser, anti-bot scripts looking for CDP artifacts come up empty. To the target site, network requests and TLS handshakes are coming from your everyday browser\u2014because they are.\n\n## Surviving Strict CSP Rules and Rate Limits\n\nRunning inside the page context introduces two classic headaches: Content-Security-Policy (\u0060connect-src\u0060) rules blocking local WebSockets, and full-page navigations wiping out your script state.\n\n### Dual-Transport IPC (WebSocket \u002B HTTP Polling Fallback)\n\nBy default, BAF communicates over full-duplex WebSockets (\u0060ws://127.0.0.1:{port}\u0060) for low-latency command execution, DOM mutation events, and streaming data.\n\nHowever, strict sites (like GitHub, banking portals, or heavily locked-down SPAs) often set CSP headers that forbid connecting to \u0060ws://127.0.0.1\u0060. When that happens, BAF\u0027s bridge catches the \u0060securitypolicyviolation\u0060 event (or detects the \u0060\u003Cmeta\u003E\u0060 policy ahead of time) and automatically drops down to HTTP polling via \u0060GM_xmlhttpRequest\u0060.\n\nIn the Chrome Extension, \u0060content_relay.js\u0060 runs in the \u0060ISOLATED\u0060 world and relays those requests to the background service worker, which has host permissions to talk to \u0060127.0.0.1\u0060 regardless of the page\u0027s CSP. Your C# code doesn\u0027t have to change a single line\u2014\u0060BrowserClient\u0060 switches its \u0060Mode\u0060 property from \u0060IpcMode.WebSocket\u0060 to \u0060IpcMode.HttpPolling\u0060 on the fly and keeps running pending commands.\n\n### In-Place Navigation and HTTP 429 Recovery\n\nWhen you navigate to a same-origin page or click a standard link, BAF\u0027s \u0060safeNavigate\u0060 function intercepts the transition, fetches the HTML with your current credentials, swaps the DOM in place, and updates \u0060window.history\u0060.\n\nThis does two things:\n\n- It keeps the WebSocket connection alive across page turns.\n- It lets BAF inspect the raw HTTP status code and headers before the browser renders the page.\n\nIf a site throws an **HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests)** during a multi-page scrape, BAF doesn\u0027t crash your run. The bridge displays a small status banner at the top of the tab, extracts the \u0060Retry-After\u0060, \u0060X-Retry-After\u0060, or \u0060RateLimit-Reset\u0060 header (whether it\u0027s in seconds, a Unix epoch, or an HTTP-date timestamp), and reports it to \u0060DataScraper\u0060 in C#. The scraper pauses until the cooldown expires, bumps up \u0060PageDelayMs\u0060 for future pages so you stop tripping the limiter, and retries the page automatically.\n\n## Dealing with React Without Losing Your Mind\n\nAnyone who has tried to automate a modern React form with \u0060document.querySelector(\u0027input\u0027).value = \u0027foo\u0027\u0060 knows that React completely ignores it. React overrides the native \u0060value\u0060 setter on the DOM node to track state internally; if you don\u0027t bypass that tracker before dispatching an \u0060input\u0060 event, the component\u0027s state resets on the next render.\n\nBAF\u0027s \u0060ReactExtensions\u0060 handle this in the bridge:\n\n- **\u0060SetReactInputValueAsync\u0060**: Grabs the native \u0060HTMLInputElement.prototype\u0060 value setter directly, applies the value (or uses \u0060document.execCommand(\u0027insertText\u0027)\u0060), and fires bubbling \u0060input\u0060 and \u0060change\u0060 events.\n- **\u0060ClickReactElementAsync\u0060**: Looks for React\u0027s internal \u0060__reactProps$\u0060 or \u0060__reactEventHandlers$\u0060 keys on the element. If it finds an \u0060onClick\u0060 handler, it invokes it with a \u0060Proxy\u0060 around the native \u0060MouseEvent\u0060 that reports \u0060isTrusted: true\u0060. If it\u0027s a standard element or SPA link, it dispatches a full \u0060pointerdown\u0060 -\u003E \u0060mousedown\u0060 -\u003E \u0060pointerup\u0060 -\u003E \u0060mouseup\u0060 -\u003E \u0060click\u0060 sequence.\n\nWriting this in C# looks like standard async code:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\nusing BrowserAutomationFramework.Extensions;\nusing BrowserAutomationFramework.Models;\nusing BrowserAutomationFramework.Server;\n\nusing var server = new AutomationServer(port: 9999);\nserver.SyncDefaultChromeExtension(IpcMode.WebSocket);\n\nserver.OnClientConnected \u002B= async (sender, client) =\u003E\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\u0022Hooked into: {client.DisplayName}\u0022);\n\n    await client.NavigateToUrlAsync(\u0022https://example.com/login\u0022);\n    await client.SetReactInputValueAsync(\u0022#email\u0022, \u0022user@example.com\u0022, overwrite: true);\n    await client.SetReactInputValueAsync(\u0022#password\u0022, \u0022hunter2\u0022, overwrite: true);\n    await client.ClickReactElementAsync(\u0022button[type=\u0027submit\u0027]\u0022);\n\n    var title = await client.EvaluateJavaScriptAsync(\u0022return document.title;\u0022);\n    Console.WriteLine($\u0022Landed on: {title.Output}\u0022);\n};\n\nawait server.StartAsync();\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\n## Structured Scraping: From Point-and-Click to POCOs\n\nSometimes you just want to pull a quick table (\u0060await client.ScrapeTableAsync(\u0022table.results\u0022)\u0060), and other times you need to crawl fifty pages of product cards with pre-scrape login sequences and regex cleanup.\n\nBAF\u0027s scraping engine (\u0060DataScraper\u0060 and the fluent \u0060ScrapeBuilder\u0060) supports 12 extraction types (\u0060InnerText\u0060, \u0060TextContent\u0060, \u0060InnerHtml\u0060, \u0060OuterHtml\u0060, \u0060Value\u0060, \u0060Attribute\u0060, \u0060Href\u0060, \u0060Src\u0060, \u0060DataAttribute\u0060, \u0060ComputedStyle\u0060, \u0060Exists\u0060, and \u0060Count\u0060).\n\nIn code, you can chain a multi-page scrape together and bind the output directly to a C# class:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\npublic class PartListing\n{\n    public string Sku { get; set; } = \u0022\u0022;\n    public decimal Price { get; set; }\n    public string ProductUrl { get; set; } = \u0022\u0022;\n}\n\nScrapeResult result = await client.CreateScrape(\u0022PartsCatalog\u0022)\n    .WithContainer(\u0022div.part-row\u0022)\n    .Field(\u0022Sku\u0022, \u0022span.sku-code\u0022, ScrapeExtractionType.InnerText)\n    .Field(\u0022Price\u0022, \u0022span.price\u0022, ScrapeExtractionType.InnerText, regexPattern: @\u0022([0-9]\u002B\\.[0-9]{2})\u0022)\n    .Field(\u0022ProductUrl\u0022, \u0022a.part-link\u0022, ScrapeExtractionType.Href)\n    .WithPagination(nextPageSelector: \u0022a.pagination-next\u0022, maxPages: 10, pageDelayMs: 1500, deduplicateRows: true)\n    .ExecuteAsync();\n\nList\u003CPartListing\u003E parts = result.BindTo\u003CPartListing\u003E();\nawait result.SaveToCsvFileAsync(\u0022parts.csv\u0022);\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nIf a site doesn\u0027t have a \u0022Next\u0022 button, you can swap \u0060.WithPagination(...)\u0060 for \u0060.WithUrlPagination(\u0022https://example.com/parts?page={page}\u0022)\u0060 or \u0060.WithPaginationSequence(customScrollSequence)\u0060 to run an \u0060AutomationSequence\u0060 between pages.\n\n## Reverse-Engineering Live Sites with \u0060SiteDumper\u0060\n\nModern web apps love to split their styles and logic across external bundles, inline \u0060\u003Cstyle\u003E\u0060 tags, CSSOM constructed stylesheets, \u0060document.adoptedStyleSheets\u0060, and dynamically fetched JS chunks that don\u0027t even appear in the initial HTML source.\n\nBAF includes a dedicated \u0060SiteDumper\u0060 engine that pulls all of these out of the live page:\n\n- **HTML (\u0060DumpHtmlAsync\u0060):** Captures the live document HTML (automatically stripping out BAF\u0027s own highlight overlays and temporary script tags) plus any accessible same-origin or cross-origin \u0060\u003Ciframe\u003E\u0060 documents.\n- **CSS (\u0060DumpCssAsync\u0060):** Fetches external \u0060\u003Clink rel=\u0022stylesheet\u0022\u003E\u0060 files, grabs inline \u0060\u003Cstyle\u003E\u0060 blocks, and serializes runtime CSSOM rules from constructed and adopted stylesheets.\n- **JavaScript (\u0060DumpJavaScriptAsync\u0060):** Collects external \u0060\u003Cscript src\u003E\u0060 files, inline script/JSON blocks, and queries \u0060performance.getEntriesByType(\u0027resource\u0027)\u0060 to catch scripts that were lazy-loaded after page startup.\n\nCalling \u0060await dump.SaveToDirectoryAsync(@\u0022C:\\SiteDump\u0022)\u0060 writes out a clean folder structure (\u0060index.html\u0060, \u0060css/\u0060, \u0060js/\u0060, \u0060combined.css\u0060, \u0060combined.js\u0060, and a full \u0060manifest.json\u0060 inventory).\n\n## BAF Studio: The Visual IDE Built on the Same Core\n\nWriting selectors by hand from DevTools gets old fast. That\u0027s why the repository includes **BAF Studio**, a dark-mode WinForms IDE built on top of \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework.dll\u0060.\n\nNotably, **100% of the logic\u2014even the layout math and virtualized tree state\u2014lives in the UI-agnostic \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework\u0060 class library.** BAF Studio is just a thin desktop front-end over \u0060AutomationStudioSession\u0060.\n\nHere is what BAF Studio gives you out of the box:\n\n### 1. Virtualized 60fps DOM Explorer \u0026 Middle-Click Picker\n\nStandard Win32 \u0060TreeView\u0060 controls choke when you feed them a 15,000-node DOM tree. BAF builds an indexed, syntax-tokenized \u0060DomTreeSnapshot\u0060 on a background thread and renders only the visible rows inside a custom virtualized control (\u0060DomTreeView\u0060).\n\n- Clicking a node in BAF Studio highlights it in your browser.\n- **Middle-clicking any element in your browser** highlights it on the page and immediately expands and scrolls to that exact node in BAF Studio\u0027s DOM tree.\n\n### 2. Automatic Selector Generalization\n\nWhen you right-click a DOM node in BAF Studio, you don\u0027t just get its raw \u0060:nth-child()\u0060 path. Using \u0060SelectorHelper\u0060, you can:\n\n- **Set as Repeating Container:** Automatically generalizes the container path (e.g., turning a specific card into a selector that matches all cards on the page) and converts your existing field selectors into \u0060:scope\u0060-relative paths.\n- **Add Repeating Field (All Similar):** Walks up the DOM snapshot to find the repeating row or structural wrapper and strips \u0060:nth-child(N)\u0060 from the right segment so a single field extracts an entire column of data.\n- **Set as Pagination Next Button:** Resolves inner \u0060\u003Cspan\u003E\u0060 or icon clicks up to the parent \u0060\u003Ca\u003E\u0060 or \u0060\u003Cbutton\u003E\u0060 and prefers resilient attributes like \u0060rel=\u0022next\u0022\u0060, \u0060aria-label\u0060, or \u0060id\u0060.\n\n### 3. Workflow \u0026 Reverse-Engineering Tabs\n\nFrom the same window, you can build multi-step \u0060AutomationSequence\u0060 lists, hook them up as Pre-Scrape or Post-Scrape actions, preview scrape results in a live table or JSON view, and run one-click HTML/CSS/JS site dumps with instant code search. Everything saves to a single, portable \u0060.json\u0060 project file that you can either run in the GUI or load headlessly in C# via \u0060AutomationProject.LoadFromFile(...)\u0060.\n\n## Extending the Bridge with Custom JS \u0026 Streaming\n\nIf the built-in actions (\u0060Click\u0060, \u0060SetValue\u0060, \u0060Navigate\u0060, \u0060EvaluateScript\u0060, \u0060Scrape\u0060, \u0060DumpSite\u0060) aren\u0027t enough, you can inject your own JavaScript handlers into the bridge via \u0060ScriptOptions.CustomScript\u0060 and stream intermediate results back to C# as they happen:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060javascript\nregisterAction(\u0027INFINITE_SCROLL\u0027, async (payload) =\u003E {\n    for (let step = 1; step \u003C= payload.maxSteps; step\u002B\u002B) {\n        window.scrollTo(0, document.body.scrollHeight);\n        await new Promise(r =\u003E setTimeout(r, 800));\n        // Stream progress back to BrowserClient.OnStreamDataReceived\n        await window.streamData(payload.taskId, { step, height: document.body.scrollHeight });\n    }\n    return \u0027Scroll finished\u0027;\n});\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nOn the C# side, you can register custom step handlers on \u0060AutomationRunner\u0060 using \u0060runner.RegisterCustomActionHandler(...)\u0060 so your custom logic plugs right into saved project sequences.\n\n## Wrapping Up \u0026 Where to Get It\n\nPlaywright and Selenium are still great for CI/CD testing where you want a clean, disposable browser environment on a build server.\n\nBut when you are scraping rate-limited data, automating workflows inside authenticated sessions, reverse-engineering a complex front-end, or dealing with aggressive bot protection that flags headless and CDP-driven browsers on sight, automating your real browser session is a much calmer way to work.\n\nTo try it out, clone the repo, run \u0060dotnet run --project \u0022BAF Studio/BAF Studio.csproj\u0022\u0060, load the auto-synced Chrome Extension folder from \u0060BrowserAutomationFramework/ChromeExtension\u0060 (or grab the Tampermonkey script for Firefox), and middle-click an element on any open tab.\n\nYou can find the source code, full API reference, and BAF Studio at either of the project repositories:\n\n- **Church of Malware Git:** [https://git.churchofmalware.org/mastercodeon/baf](https://www.google.com/url?sa=E\u0026q=https%3A%2F%2Fgit.churchofmalware.org%2Fmastercodeon%2Fbaf)\n- **GitHub:** [https://github.com/codeon314/baf](https://www.google.com/url?sa=E\u0026q=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcodeon314%2Fbaf)",
      "Diffs": []
    },
    {
      "Id": "1cada29a",
      "Timestamp": "2026-10-28T02:58:20.7372069-05:00",
      "UserPrompt": "Perfect! Now write a short description for the article.",
      "ModelResponse": "Anti-Agent v1.0\n\nBelow is a short description for the article, written in a natural, developer-focused tone suitable for a blog subtitle, meta description, or post summary. No project files were modified.\n\n**Article Description:**\n\nTired of playing whack-a-mole with Cloudflare Turnstile, CDP leaks, and TLS fingerprinting in Playwright and Puppeteer? Browser Automation Framework (BAF) takes a different approach to .NET 10 browser automation and web scraping: instead of launching an instrumented browser binary, it hooks directly into your everyday browser session via a lightweight WebSocket/HTTP IPC bridge. Here is a look under the hood at how BAF and BAF Studio handle React state, strict CSP rules, HTTP 429 rate limits, and live site reverse-engineering without tripping anti-bot checks.",
      "ModelExplanation": "Below is a short description for the article, written in a natural, developer-focused tone suitable for a blog subtitle, meta description, or post summary. No project files were modified.\n\n**Article Description:**\n\nTired of playing whack-a-mole with Cloudflare Turnstile, CDP leaks, and TLS fingerprinting in Playwright and Puppeteer? Browser Automation Framework (BAF) takes a different approach to .NET 10 browser automation and web scraping: instead of launching an instrumented browser binary, it hooks directly into your everyday browser session via a lightweight WebSocket/HTTP IPC bridge. Here is a look under the hood at how BAF and BAF Studio handle React state, strict CSP rules, HTTP 429 rate limits, and live site reverse-engineering without tripping anti-bot checks.",
      "Diffs": []
    },
